fuse-7z-ng is licensed under the GNU GPL v3. (See COPYING for more information)

Usage
=====

Mounting an archive with fuse-7z-ng is like mounting another typical FUSE
filesystem:
$ fuse-7z-ng --automount foobar.zip ~/mount

Note: --automount makes the target folder if not present.

Then do something with the mounted file system, and unmount:

$ fusermount3 -u ~/mount

Options:

    -v, --verbose   print debug messages
    -f              stay in the foreground
    -d              FUSE debug output, implies -f and --verbose
    --syslog        log to syslog instead of stderr
    -o OPT[,OPT...] FUSE mount options, see mount.fuse3(8)

The file system is always mounted read-only.

The archive is listed once when it is mounted. A member is extracted
by the 7-Zip tool when it is opened, into an unlinked temporary file in
$TMPDIR (/tmp by default), and the file is dropped when the last open
handle of the member is closed. Keep this in mind for large members if
/tmp is a tmpfs.

Archive members are untrusted: members with absolute names or ".."
components, symbolic and hard links, devices, FIFOs and sockets are not
shown, and their number is printed at mount time. Permissions and
modification times are taken from the archive when it stores them.

Requirements
============

* Linux (or another system with FUSE 3 and posix_spawn)
* libfuse 3.2 or newer and fusermount3
* one of the 7-Zip command line tools, found at run time in PATH:
  7zz (7-Zip), 7z (7-Zip or p7zip) or 7za.
  Set FUSE_7Z_PROGRAM to use another one, for example
  FUSE_7Z_PROGRAM=/opt/7zip/7zz.

fuse-7z-ng does not link with lib7zip or p7zip anymore; the 7-Zip tool
is run as a separate process without a shell.

Building
========

You need CMake 3.12 or newer, a C++17 compiler, pkg-config and the
FUSE 3 development files, for example:

  Debian, Ubuntu:  apt install cmake g++ pkg-config libfuse3-dev fuse3 7zip
  Fedora:          dnf install cmake gcc-c++ pkgconf fuse3-devel fuse3 7zip

Then:

$ cmake -S . -B build
$ cmake --build build
$ sudo cmake --install build

Tests
=====

$ ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

* listing, sevenzip: unit tests of the 7-Zip output parser, the checks
  of member names and how the 7-Zip tool is started. They need nothing
  but the compiler.
* backend: lists and extracts real 7z and zip archives with the 7-Zip
  tool, skipped without it.
* integration: mounts archives with FUSE and compares them with their
  source files, checks that unsafe members are not shown. It is skipped
  without /dev/fuse, fusermount3 or the 7-Zip tool, for example in
  build chroots and containers.

Configure with -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF to skip building the tests.

Status
======

Only reads are supported, and there are no plans for writes: there is
no sane way to update big archives in place.

Note that extension auto-detection (by magic) seems to have issues,
but you can work around that by creating symlinks with proper extensions::
$ ln -sf "[Contents]" $TMP/initrd-contents.cpio
$ fuse-7z-ng --automount $TMP/initrd-contents.cpio ~/mnt/initrd-contents

Encrypted archives are not supported yet: 7-Zip is given an empty
password, so encrypted members can not be read.
